# serializer version: 1
# name: test_adopt_plugin_publish_golden
  '''
  role: standalone
  summary: Declare a Claude Code plugin in an existing repo — merge settings, append CLAUDE.md, commit.
  params:
    runActivity: "run-claude"
    clonePath: ""
    agentId: "claude-agent"
  steps:
    - id: worktree
      activity: create-worktree
      input:
        agentId: "{{params.agentId}}"
        clonePath: "{{params.clonePath}}"
        checkout:
          kind: branch
          branch: "feature/{{params.slug}}"
    - id: setup
      activity: setup
      input:
        agentId: "{{params.agentId}}"
        setup:
          - cmd: "mkdir -p ~/.claude/skills && cp -rn \"${H_SKILLS_DIR:?H_SKILLS_DIR must be set to the h skills root}\"/. ~/.claude/skills/"
          - cmd: "if [ -f \"${H_RULES_DIR:-}\"/h-runtime.md ]; then bash \"${H_SKILLS_DIR:?H_SKILLS_DIR must be set to the h skills root}\"/install-steering.sh \"$H_RULES_DIR\"/h-runtime.md; fi"
    - id: adopt
      activity: "{{params.runActivity}}"
      input:
        outputContract: {"properties":{"settingsPath":{"description":"repo-relative path of the .claude/settings.json file as written (e.g. .claude/settings.json)","type":"string"},"steeringPath":{"description":"repo-relative path of the CLAUDE.md file as written (e.g. CLAUDE.md)","type":"string"},"summary":{"description":"what was done — which keys/plugins were already present and preserved, and a reminder that declaring is not installing: each checkout must still run `claude plugin install \u003cplugin\u003e@\u003cmarketplaceKey\u003e --scope project`","type":"string"}},"required":["settingsPath","steeringPath","summary"],"type":"object"}
        cwd: "{{worktree.worktreePath}}"
        task: |-
          You are in a git worktree of the target repository, on branch feature/{{params.slug}}.
          Your task is to declare a Claude Code plugin in this repo. Follow these instructions exactly.
  
          The five inputs you need:
            marketplaceKey:  {{params.marketplaceKey}}
            marketplaceRepo: {{params.marketplaceRepo}}
            plugin:          {{params.plugin}}
            conventionNote:  {{params.conventionNote}}
  
          1. Read `.claude/settings.json` if it exists (treat as `{}` when absent). Print the full
             file contents BEFORE making any change (or print "file absent" when it does not exist).
             Then merge ADDITIVELY:
             - Ensure `extraKnownMarketplaces` contains the key `{{params.marketplaceKey}}` with value
               `{"source": "github", "repo": "{{params.marketplaceRepo}}"}`.
             - Ensure `enabledPlugins` contains the key `{{params.plugin}}@{{params.marketplaceKey}}` with
               value `true`.
             Preserve every other top-level key, every other entry inside `extraKnownMarketplaces`
             and `enabledPlugins`, and the file's existing formatting choices (2-space JSON indentation,
             trailing newline). When the file is absent, create it with only `extraKnownMarketplaces`
             and `enabledPlugins`. Write the result and print the full file AFTER the change.
  
          2. Append the convention note to the repo's `CLAUDE.md` (create it if absent):
             - If the note's first line begins with `#` (a Markdown heading), append it verbatim
               after one blank line from the last line of the existing file.
             - Otherwise, append it under a new `## {{params.plugin}}` heading (one blank line, then the
               heading, one blank line, then the note body).
             Never edit any existing section of CLAUDE.md.
  
          3. `git add` both files (`.claude/settings.json` and `CLAUDE.md`) and commit on the current
             branch with the exact subject line:
               chore: adopt the {{params.plugin}} plugin
             Do NOT add any Co-Authored-By or other attribution trailers — the target repo's commit
             conventions govern, and adding trailers here would violate them.
  
          4. Report a structured block with exactly these three fields:
             - `settingsPath`: the repo-relative path of the settings file as written
               (e.g. `.claude/settings.json`)
             - `steeringPath`: the repo-relative path of the CLAUDE.md file as written
               (e.g. `CLAUDE.md`)
             - `summary`: one paragraph stating (a) which keys and plugins were ALREADY present and
               have been preserved unchanged, and (b) that DECLARING is not INSTALLING — each checkout
               that wants the plugin's tools must still run:
                 claude plugin install {{params.plugin}}@{{params.marketplaceKey}} --scope project
               (trxy carried declared entries from 2026-08-13 and the plugin never loaded once because
               the install step was skipped; this reminder is mandatory so the operator does not repeat
               that mistake)
          
          ===OUTPUT CONTRACT===
          End your final message with a fenced ```json code block containing a single JSON
          object matching this schema. The block is machine-validated: a missing or
          mismatching block fails this step. Nothing may follow the block.
          
          MANDATORY — the block MUST carry `settingsPath`, `steeringPath`, `summary`.
          Omitting any one of them fails the step even when the
          work itself succeeded, and a long run is exactly when this gets forgotten: on 2026-08-22 a
          revise-pr run rebased a branch, fixed five review findings, replied, resolved the threads and
          force-pushed — 28 minutes and $7.93 — then omitted one required field and was reported as a
          failed step. Re-read this line before you write the block.
          {
            "properties": {
              "settingsPath": {
                "description": "repo-relative path of the .claude/settings.json file as written (e.g. .claude/settings.json)",
                "type": "string"
              },
              "steeringPath": {
                "description": "repo-relative path of the CLAUDE.md file as written (e.g. CLAUDE.md)",
                "type": "string"
              },
              "summary": {
                "description": "what was done — which keys/plugins were already present and preserved, and a reminder that declaring is not installing: each checkout must still run `claude plugin install \u003cplugin\u003e@\u003cmarketplaceKey\u003e --scope project`",
                "type": "string"
              }
            },
            "required": [
              "settingsPath",
              "steeringPath",
              "summary"
            ],
            "type": "object"
          }
  outputs:
    type: object
    required:
      - settingsPath
      - steeringPath
      - summary
    properties:
      settingsPath:
        type: string
        description: >-
          repo-relative path of the .claude/settings.json file as written (e.g. .claude/settings.json)
      steeringPath:
        type: string
        description: >-
          repo-relative path of the CLAUDE.md file as written (e.g. CLAUDE.md)
      summary:
        type: string
        description: >-
          what was done — which keys/plugins were already present and preserved, and a reminder
          that declaring is not installing: each checkout must still run
          `claude plugin install <plugin>@<marketplaceKey> --scope project`
  
  '''
# ---
# name: test_answer_publish_golden
  '''
  role: standalone
  summary: Answer one task with a validated structured answer — the panelizable atom.
  params:
    runActivity: "run-claude"
    agentId: "claude-agent"
  steps:
    - id: answer
      activity: "{{params.runActivity}}"
      input:
        outputContract: {"properties":{"answer":{"description":"the conclusive answer to the task, plainly stated","type":"string"},"disagreements":{"description":"panel runs only — where independent answers diverged and why it matters; empty or omitted otherwise","type":"string"}},"required":["answer"],"type":"object"}
        task: |-
          Answer the task under ===TASK=== below. Work independently from your own knowledge and
          reasoning; do not ask questions. Be concise and conclusive — end with your answer,
          plainly stated, reported via the fields of your final json block.
          
          ===OUTPUT CONTRACT===
          End your final message with a fenced ```json code block containing a single JSON
          object matching this schema. The block is machine-validated: a missing or
          mismatching block fails this step. Nothing may follow the block.
          
          MANDATORY — the block MUST carry `answer`.
          Omitting it fails the step even when the
          work itself succeeded, and a long run is exactly when this gets forgotten: on 2026-08-22 a
          revise-pr run rebased a branch, fixed five review findings, replied, resolved the threads and
          force-pushed — 28 minutes and $7.93 — then omitted one required field and was reported as a
          failed step. Re-read this line before you write the block.
          {
            "properties": {
              "answer": {
                "description": "the conclusive answer to the task, plainly stated",
                "type": "string"
              },
              "disagreements": {
                "description": "panel runs only — where independent answers diverged and why it matters; empty or omitted otherwise",
                "type": "string"
              }
            },
            "required": [
              "answer"
            ],
            "type": "object"
          }
  
          ===TASK===
          {{params.task}}
  outputs:
    type: object
    required:
      - answer
    properties:
      answer:
        type: string
        description: the conclusive answer to the task, plainly stated
      disagreements:
        type: string
        description: >-
          panel runs only — where independent answers diverged and why it matters; empty or
          omitted otherwise
  panelSynthesis: |-
    Merge the panelists' answers into ONE conclusive `answer`: prefer the strongest reasoning
    over the largest headcount, and merge complementary points rather than picking a side when
    they compose. Surface real divergences — different conclusions, not different phrasings —
    in `disagreements`, naming which panelist held what and why it matters; leave it empty
    when the panel genuinely agrees.
  
  '''
# ---
# name: test_arm_revise_golden
  '''
  role: overlay
  summary: Overlay — arm the per-PR revise cron at PR birth (the arm-* closing step).
  steps:
    - id: arm-revise-pr
      activity: register-cron
      input:
        # The saved workflow the cron re-fires each due tick until the PR merges (revise reports
        # a structured `goal: RESOLVED` on merge; the cron engine reads wf:*.resolved and deactivates).
        workflow: revise-pr
        repo: "{{params.repo}}"
        slug: "{{params.slug}}"
        cadence: "0 */6 * * *"
        maxFires: 20
        # Only arm if the PR actually opened: register-cron reads the `pr` field of create-pr's output json block.
        requirePrFrom: "{{create-pr.output}}"
  
  '''
# ---
# name: test_bootstrap_repo_publish_golden
  '''
  role: standalone
  summary: Create and scaffold a new repository, verify it, and push.
  params:
    runActivity: "run-claude"
    agentId: "claude-agent"
    visibility: "private"
  steps:
    - id: setup
      activity: setup
      input:
        agentId: "{{params.agentId}}"
        setup:
          - cmd: "mkdir -p ~/.claude/skills && cp -rn \"${H_SKILLS_DIR:?H_SKILLS_DIR must be set to the h skills root}\"/. ~/.claude/skills/"
          - cmd: "if [ -f \"${H_RULES_DIR:-}\"/h-runtime.md ]; then bash \"${H_SKILLS_DIR:?H_SKILLS_DIR must be set to the h skills root}\"/install-steering.sh \"$H_RULES_DIR\"/h-runtime.md; fi"
          - cmd: "bash ~/.claude/skills/install-plugins.sh \"{{params.plugins}}\" https://github.com/stiproot/hex-arch https://github.com/stiproot/plan-management https://github.com/stiproot/code-comprehension"
    - id: bootstrap
      activity: "{{params.runActivity}}"
      input:
        outputContract: {"properties":{"defaultBranch":{"description":"the default branch name (main) (required on CREATED, omitted on FAILED)","type":"string"},"repoUrl":{"description":"the HTML URL of the created repository (required on CREATED, omitted on FAILED)","type":"string"},"result":{"description":"CREATED when the repository was created, committed, and pushed; FAILED otherwise","enum":["CREATED","FAILED"]},"skipped":{"description":"present on FAILED — why the run halted (repo already exists, gate failure, GH_TOKEN unset, verifyCmd not provided, ...)","type":"string"},"verify":{"description":"the acceptance-gate outcome; PASS only when gate ran and passed; omitted if gate was skipped","enum":["PASS","FAIL"]}},"required":["result"],"type":"object"}
        task: |-
          Your job is to create a new GitHub repository named {{params.repoName}} and bootstrap it
          from the spec below. Work in your current directory (the agent workspace). There is no
          pre-cloned repo — you are building from scratch.
  
          ===SPEC===
          {{params.spec}}
  
          Follow this procedure exactly, in order. Stop immediately on any failure and report via
          the final json block.
  
          1. CREATE the repository.
             Use the github MCP tool create_repository:
               name: {{params.repoName}}
               visibility: {{params.visibility}}
             Set the tool's `private` parameter to true when the visibility value above is
             "private" (or empty), and to false only when it is exactly "public".
             If the repository already exists (the tool returns an error indicating the name is
             taken), STOP. Report `result: FAILED` and `skipped: "repo already exists"`. Do not
             adopt, modify, or push to an existing repository.
             On success: note the returned `html_url` as `repoUrl` and the `default_branch` as
             `defaultBranch` for your final json block.
  
          2. SCAFFOLD the project.
             In your current working directory:
               git init
             Implement the project as described under ===SPEC=== above. Create all files needed to
             satisfy the spec. Do not create files outside the spec.
  
          3. VERIFY.
             If verifyCmd is empty or not provided, STOP. Report `result: FAILED` and
             `skipped: "verifyCmd not provided"`. Do not proceed without a verification gate.
             Otherwise, run the acceptance command exactly as given (no modifications, no weakening):
               {{params.verifyCmd}}
             If it fails, fix forward and retry up to 3 times. If it still fails after 3 attempts,
             STOP. Report `result: FAILED`, `verify: FAIL`, and `skipped: "verify gate failed after
             3 attempts"`. Do not push a repository that does not pass the gate.
             On success: note `verify: PASS` for your final json block.
  
          4. COMMIT and PUSH.
             Stage and commit files individually (never `git add -A` or `git commit -a`):
               git add <file>   # repeat for each file
             Use a single conventional commit message summarising the scaffold.
             Set the default branch:
               git branch -M main
             Push with a one-shot token URL — extract <owner> SAFELY from the creation response or
             your GitHub username. The owner must be URL-safe; if extracting from `git remote get-url origin`,
             extract the segment between github.com/ and the repo name, e.g. from
             https://github.com/octocat/repo.git → octocat. Do not include special characters or slashes.
             The repo name is {{params.repoName}}:
               git remote add origin "https://github.com/<owner>/{{params.repoName}}.git"
               git push "https://x-access-token:${GH_TOKEN}@github.com/<owner>/{{params.repoName}}.git" main
             Never write the token into git config or the remote URL permanently.
             If GH_TOKEN is unset, STOP. Report `result: FAILED` and
             `skipped: "GH_TOKEN is not set"`.
  
          Report the outcome ONLY via the fields of your final json block below. There is no other
          reporting channel.
          
          ===OUTPUT CONTRACT===
          End your final message with a fenced ```json code block containing a single JSON
          object matching this schema. The block is machine-validated: a missing or
          mismatching block fails this step. Nothing may follow the block.
          
          MANDATORY — the block MUST carry `result`.
          Omitting it fails the step even when the
          work itself succeeded, and a long run is exactly when this gets forgotten: on 2026-08-22 a
          revise-pr run rebased a branch, fixed five review findings, replied, resolved the threads and
          force-pushed — 28 minutes and $7.93 — then omitted one required field and was reported as a
          failed step. Re-read this line before you write the block.
          {
            "properties": {
              "defaultBranch": {
                "description": "the default branch name (main) (required on CREATED, omitted on FAILED)",
                "type": "string"
              },
              "repoUrl": {
                "description": "the HTML URL of the created repository (required on CREATED, omitted on FAILED)",
                "type": "string"
              },
              "result": {
                "description": "CREATED when the repository was created, committed, and pushed; FAILED otherwise",
                "enum": [
                  "CREATED",
                  "FAILED"
                ]
              },
              "skipped": {
                "description": "present on FAILED — why the run halted (repo already exists, gate failure, GH_TOKEN unset, verifyCmd not provided, ...)",
                "type": "string"
              },
              "verify": {
                "description": "the acceptance-gate outcome; PASS only when gate ran and passed; omitted if gate was skipped",
                "enum": [
                  "PASS",
                  "FAIL"
                ]
              }
            },
            "required": [
              "result"
            ],
            "type": "object"
          }
  outputs:
    type: object
    required:
      - result
    properties:
      result:
        enum:
          - CREATED
          - FAILED
        description: CREATED when the repository was created, committed, and pushed; FAILED otherwise
      repoUrl:
        type: string
        description: the HTML URL of the created repository (required on CREATED, omitted on FAILED)
      defaultBranch:
        type: string
        description: the default branch name (main) (required on CREATED, omitted on FAILED)
      verify:
        enum:
          - PASS
          - FAIL
        description: the acceptance-gate outcome; PASS only when gate ran and passed; omitted if gate was skipped
      skipped:
        type: string
        description: present on FAILED — why the run halted (repo already exists, gate failure, GH_TOKEN unset, verifyCmd not provided, ...)
  
  '''
# ---
# name: test_create_pr_golden
  '''
  role: overlay
  summary: Overlay — append the push / open-PR / respond-to-review finishing step.
  params:
    issueNumber: ""
    baseBranch: ""
  steps:
    - id: create-pr
      activity: "{{params.runActivity}}"
      input:
        cwd: "{{worktree.worktreePath}}"
        model: "{{params.modelImplement}}"
        outputContract: {"properties":{"base":{"description":"the PR's base branch AS GITHUB REPORTS IT after opening/updating (read it back from the PR, never echo the requested one) — a driver asserts it against the campaign's branch","type":"string"},"head":{"description":"the PR's head branch as GitHub reports it","type":"string"},"pr":{"description":"the pull request number, when a PR exists (opened, updated, or pre-existing)","type":"integer"},"skipped":{"description":"present INSTEAD of pr/url — why no PR exists (verify gate failed, auth failure, nothing to push, ...)","type":"string"},"url":{"description":"the pull request URL","type":"string"}},"type":"object"}
        task: |-
          ===ISSUE===
          {{params.issueNumber}}
  
          The implementation step has committed the changes locally on branch feature/{{params.slug}}.
          Read plan-feature-{{params.slug}}.md in the worktree root and the local commit on
          feature/{{params.slug}} to understand what the implementation step did — use these as your
          sources for the PR title and body. Now push and open (or update) a pull request.
          1. Push the branch. The origin remote has no credentials — derive <owner>/<repo>
             from `git remote get-url origin` and push with a one-shot token URL:
             git push "https://x-access-token:${GH_TOKEN}@github.com/<owner>/<repo>.git" feature/{{params.slug}}
             Never write the token into git config or the remote URL; if GH_TOKEN is unset,
             skip the push and explain why in the `skipped` field of your final json block.
          2. Open a pull request with the github MCP tool create_pull_request. Its BASE is
             `{{params.baseBranch}}` — when that is empty use the
             repository's default branch, and when it names a branch pass it as the tool's
             `base` argument so the PR targets that branch instead. Do not retarget an
             existing PR you did not open in this run.
             Title from the feature, body from the plan file
             and the implementation commit (summarize what the implementation step committed).
             Include the `===VERIFY EVIDENCE===` block below in the PR body VERBATIM, as a
             fenced json block under an "## Evidence" heading, when it is non-empty (verify
             was composed in): it is the implementation step's validated report — baseline
             and final exit codes and failure counts, the gate verdict, every extra check —
             and a driver reads those numbers from the PR, not from your prose. Do not
             summarise or reformat it, and do not omit it (2026-09-02: a run did every check
             the spec asked for and the PR body carried none of it).
             Everything the implementation step OBSERVED that belongs in the PR is in that
             block — including any `demonstrations` (a check deliberately made to fail, with
             the lines it printed). Never write out command output you did not run in THIS
             step: if the spec asked for a demonstration and the block carries none, say so
             in one line rather than reconstructing what it probably printed (2026-09-03: a
             PR body quoted a failing test by the wrong number, at the wrong line, in a
             format the runner does not emit — none of it had happened).
             Include the `===ITEST EVIDENCE===` section below in the PR body when those values
             are non-empty (i.e. when run-itest was composed in and ran).
             Pass the intended Markdown directly as the tool's body value, beginning with the intended heading or first line;
             do not wrap it in shell command substitution or a heredoc.
             If the value under ===ISSUE=== above is a number N, include the line
             `Closes #N` on its own line in the PR body, so the merge auto-closes that issue;
             otherwise omit it. If a PR for this branch already exists, your push has updated
             it — do not open a duplicate.
             AFTER opening or finding the PR, read it back (pull_request_read) and report
             its `base` and `head` branches from that response in your json block. Never
             copy the requested base into the block: the value must be what GitHub holds,
             because a PR that landed against the wrong branch is exactly what the field
             exists to catch (2026-09-01: the base was in prose only, and a driver could
             not tell a retargeted PR from a correct one without opening each).
  
          ===VERIFY EVIDENCE===
          {{implement.structured}}
  
          ===ITEST EVIDENCE===
          class: {{itest.class}}
          treeHash: {{itest.treeHash}}
          durationMs: {{itest.durationMs}}
          outputTail: {{itest.outputTail}}
          Include this evidence section in the PR body only when these values are non-empty
          (itest was composed in and ran).
  
          3. If a PR for this branch already existed before this push (this is a revise
             run, not a first-time creation), respond to review feedback:
             a. Find the PR number: use the github MCP tool to list pull requests by
               head branch feature/{{params.slug}}.
             b. Fetch UNRESOLVED review threads: pull_request_read with method
               get_review_comments on that PR. Each thread has an isResolved field, a
               thread node id (PRRT_...), and comments with numeric ids.
             c. For each unresolved thread that this run actually addressed, reply inline
               with add_reply_to_pull_request_comment (body: "Addressed in <sha>:
               <what changed>"), then resolve it with pull_request_review_write method
               resolve_thread passing the thread node id.
             d. Do NOT reply to or resolve threads this run did not address.
             e. Never APPROVE or REQUEST_CHANGES on the PR.
          Report the PR (number + URL) — or why it was skipped — ONLY via the fields of
          your final json block below; there is no other reporting channel.
          
          ===OUTPUT CONTRACT===
          End your final message with a fenced ```json code block containing a single JSON
          object matching this schema. The block is machine-validated: a missing or
          mismatching block fails this step. Nothing may follow the block.
          {
            "properties": {
              "base": {
                "description": "the PR's base branch AS GITHUB REPORTS IT after opening/updating (read it back from the PR, never echo the requested one) — a driver asserts it against the campaign's branch",
                "type": "string"
              },
              "head": {
                "description": "the PR's head branch as GitHub reports it",
                "type": "string"
              },
              "pr": {
                "description": "the pull request number, when a PR exists (opened, updated, or pre-existing)",
                "type": "integer"
              },
              "skipped": {
                "description": "present INSTEAD of pr/url — why no PR exists (verify gate failed, auth failure, nothing to push, ...)",
                "type": "string"
              },
              "url": {
                "description": "the pull request URL",
                "type": "string"
              }
            },
            "type": "object"
          }
  outputs:
    type: object
    properties:
      pr:
        type: integer
        description: the pull request number, when a PR exists (opened, updated, or pre-existing)
      url:
        type: string
        description: the pull request URL
      base:
        type: string
        description: the PR's base branch AS GITHUB REPORTS IT after opening/updating (read it back from the PR, never echo the requested one) — a driver asserts it against the campaign's branch
      head:
        type: string
        description: the PR's head branch as GitHub reports it
      skipped:
        type: string
        description: present INSTEAD of pr/url — why no PR exists (verify gate failed, auth failure, nothing to push, ...)
  
  '''
# ---
# name: test_gc_worktrees_golden
  '''
  role: standalone
  summary: Collect the shared-workspace worktrees h has finished with.
  params:
    agentId: "claude-agent"
    pruneUntracked: "false"
    minAgeMs: "86400000"
    keep: ""
    dryRun: "false"
  steps:
    - id: gc
      activity: gc-worktrees
      input:
        agentId: "{{params.agentId}}"
        pruneUntracked: "{{params.pruneUntracked}}"
        minAgeMs: "{{params.minAgeMs}}"
        keep: "{{params.keep}}"
        dryRun: "{{params.dryRun}}"
        traceparent: "{{traceparent}}"
  
  '''
# ---
# name: test_implement_publish_mode_golden
  '''
  role: base
  summary: Cut a worktree, plan read-only, then implement a spec (commits stay local).
  params:
    runActivity: "run-claude"
    clonePath: ""
    remoteBase: ""
    agentId: "claude-agent"
    modelPlan: "claude-sonnet-4-6"
    modelImplement: "claude-haiku-4-5"
  steps:
    - id: worktree
      activity: create-worktree
      input:
        agentId: "{{params.agentId}}"
        clonePath: "{{params.clonePath}}"
        checkout:
          kind: branch
          branch: "feature/{{params.slug}}"
          remoteBase: "{{params.remoteBase}}"
    - id: setup
      activity: setup
      input:
        agentId: "{{params.agentId}}"
        setup:
          - cmd: "mkdir -p ~/.claude/skills && cp -rn \"${H_SKILLS_DIR:?H_SKILLS_DIR must be set to the h skills root}\"/. ~/.claude/skills/"
          - cmd: "if [ -f \"${H_RULES_DIR:-}\"/h-runtime.md ]; then bash \"${H_SKILLS_DIR:?H_SKILLS_DIR must be set to the h skills root}\"/install-steering.sh \"$H_RULES_DIR\"/h-runtime.md; fi"
    - id: plan
      activity: "{{params.runActivity}}"
      input:
        cwd: "{{worktree.worktreePath}}"
        model: "{{params.modelPlan}}"
        permissionMode: plan
        task: |-
          You are in a git worktree of the target repository, on branch feature/{{params.slug}}.
  
          First check whether a prior run left anything — you may be a RESUMPTION, and
          re-planning finished work is the most expensive mistake available to you. Three
          places, in order, because they survive different things:
          1. `git log` on this branch. The most reliable: a re-fire reuses the branch, so a
             prior run's COMMITS are here even when its worktree is not.
          2. plan-feature-{{params.slug}}.md in the worktree root. Deliberately uncommitted, so it
             is present when the worktree was reused and ABSENT when a fresh one was cut —
             read it if it is there, and do not conclude from its absence that nothing was done.
          3. ONLY if the dapr MCP is wired here: actor_state_get with actorId
             'feature-{{params.slug}}' and key 'plan'. That is where a prior run's plan text
             survives a fresh worktree; on the LOCAL substrate there are no services and so no
             dapr MCP, and (1) plus (2) are the whole story — skipping this is EXPECTED, not a
             degradation.
          If what you find still matches the spec and the code, reuse it instead of
          re-investigating. Verify a claim cheaply against the tree before trusting it.
  
          Otherwise investigate the feature request under ===FEATURE SPEC=== below: explore the
          relevant code and produce an implementation plan. Do NOT write code or modify any
          files. If the feature already appears implemented, say so in the plan.
          End your response with the plan under the exact marker ===IMPLEMENTATION PLAN===.
  
          ===FEATURE SPEC===
          {{params.spec}}
    - id: implement
      activity: "{{params.runActivity}}"
      input:
        cwd: "{{worktree.worktreePath}}"
        model: "{{params.modelImplement}}"
        task: |-
          You are in a git worktree of the target repository, on branch feature/{{params.slug}}.
  
          First persist the plan you were handed under ===IMPLEMENTATION PLAN=== (the
          shared-context handoff, so a re-run or another session can pick it up — the planning
          agent runs read-only and cannot write it itself):
          1. Write it to plan-feature-{{params.slug}}.md in the worktree root. This is the handoff
             and it is required. It is PER-RUN CONTEXT, not a tracking log: do NOT give it a
             progress/status section. If the repository keeps its own plan or tracking doc for
             this work, THAT doc is the authoritative record of what is done and what is next
             — read it first and keep it current; this file only carries the plan itself. (A
             scratch file carrying its own empty "Progress log" heading has shadowed a repo's
             real one and cost a whole pass, because the reader saw an empty log and concluded
             nothing had been done.)
          2. ONLY if the dapr MCP is wired in this environment, ALSO call actor_state_set with
             actorId 'feature-{{params.slug}}' and key 'plan', value the plan text — durable in the
             state store, inspectable from another session via actor_state_get. On the LOCAL
             substrate there are no services and so no dapr MCP: the file from (1) sits on the
             operator's own disk and is the whole handoff there, so skipping this is EXPECTED,
             not a degradation — do not attempt the call and do not report its absence.
          If (1) fails, or (2) fails while the tool IS present, do NOT fail the run — but you
          MUST say so explicitly in your final output, so a real degradation stays visible.
  
          Then implement the feature described under ===FEATURE SPEC=== below, following the
          plan. If the feature already appears implemented, make no changes and say so.
          COMMIT AS YOU GO on branch feature/{{params.slug}}, at a sensible cadence — after each
          coherent unit of work (a migration, a module, one component), never after every file
          edit. Add files individually (never `git add -A` or `git commit -a`), and leave
          plan-feature-{{params.slug}}.md uncommitted (run context, not feature code).
          Do NOT push the branch and do NOT open a pull request — this run stops at the local
          branch, and the operator decides what happens to it.
          (Committing costs the reader nothing: this is a worktree, so the change is
          `git diff <base>...HEAD` either way. Leaving it uncommitted only meant that a step
          killed at its wall-clock deadline lost everything it had paid for.)
  
          ===FEATURE SPEC===
          {{params.spec}}
  
          ===IMPLEMENTATION PLAN===
          {{plan.output}}
  
  '''
