# name: test_implement_yaml_golden
  '''
  role: base
  summary: Cut a worktree, plan read-only, then implement a spec (commits stay local).
  instanceId: "feature-hostile-fixture"
  steps:
    - id: worktree
      activity: create-worktree
      input:
        agentId: "claude-agent"
        clonePath: "{{params.clonePath}}"
        checkout:
          kind: branch
          branch: "feature/hostile-fixture"
          remoteBase: "{{params.remoteBase}}"
    - id: setup
      activity: setup
      input:
        agentId: "claude-agent"
        setup:
          - cmd: "mkdir -p ~/.claude/skills && cp -rn \"${H_SKILLS_DIR:?H_SKILLS_DIR must be set to the h skills root}\"/. ~/.claude/skills/"
          - cmd: "if [ -f \"${H_RULES_DIR:-}\"/h-runtime.md ]; then bash \"${H_SKILLS_DIR:?H_SKILLS_DIR must be set to the h skills root}\"/install-steering.sh \"$H_RULES_DIR\"/h-runtime.md; fi"
    - id: plan
      activity: run-claude
      input:
        cwd: "{{worktree.worktreePath}}"
        model: "claude-sonnet-4-6"
        permissionMode: plan
        task: |-
          You are in a git worktree of the target repository, on branch feature/hostile-fixture.
  
          First check whether a prior run left anything — you may be a RESUMPTION, and
          re-planning finished work is the most expensive mistake available to you. Three
          places, in order, because they survive different things:
          1. `git log` on this branch. The most reliable: a re-fire reuses the branch, so a
             prior run's COMMITS are here even when its worktree is not.
          2. plan-feature-hostile-fixture.md in the worktree root. Deliberately uncommitted, so it
             is present when the worktree was reused and ABSENT when a fresh one was cut —
             read it if it is there, and do not conclude from its absence that nothing was done.
          3. ONLY if the dapr MCP is wired here: actor_state_get with actorId
             'feature-hostile-fixture' and key 'plan'. That is where a prior run's plan text
             survives a fresh worktree; on the LOCAL substrate there are no services and so no
             dapr MCP, and (1) plus (2) are the whole story — skipping this is EXPECTED, not a
             degradation.
          If what you find still matches the spec and the code, reuse it instead of
          re-investigating. Verify a claim cheaply against the tree before trusting it.
  
          Otherwise investigate the feature request under ===FEATURE SPEC=== below: explore the
          relevant code and produce an implementation plan. Do NOT write code or modify any
          files. If the feature already appears implemented, say so in the plan.
          End your response with the plan under the exact marker ===IMPLEMENTATION PLAN===.
  
          ===FEATURE SPEC===
          # Hostile-content fixture
          
          This spec deliberately carries every token class that must survive rendering untouched.
          It is the committed proof of the delimiter-coexistence story — if any layer (helm, YAML,
          the JSON wire step) mangles one of these, the golden snapshot diff catches it.
          
          - agent-side shell vars: $AGENT_APP_DIR and ${H_SKILLS_DIR}
          - envsubst-style tokens: ${VARS} and ${NOT_A_REAL_VAR}
          - engine-style tokens: {{step.field}} and {"$ref": "step.field"}
          - "double quotes", 'single quotes', back\slashes, a lone $ sign
          - markdown: `inline code`, **bold**, a [link](https://example.invalid)
          
            an indented block
              a deeper one
          
          Trailing punctuation and unicode: café, emoji ✅, em-dash —
          
    - id: implement
      activity: run-claude
      input:
        cwd: "{{worktree.worktreePath}}"
        model: "claude-haiku-4-5"
        task: |-
          You are in a git worktree of the target repository, on branch feature/hostile-fixture.
  
          First persist the plan you were handed under ===IMPLEMENTATION PLAN=== (the
          shared-context handoff, so a re-run or another session can pick it up — the planning
          agent runs read-only and cannot write it itself):
          1. Write it to plan-feature-hostile-fixture.md in the worktree root. This is the handoff
             and it is required. It is PER-RUN CONTEXT, not a tracking log: do NOT give it a
             progress/status section. If the repository keeps its own plan or tracking doc for
             this work, THAT doc is the authoritative record of what is done and what is next
             — read it first and keep it current; this file only carries the plan itself. (A
             scratch file carrying its own empty "Progress log" heading has shadowed a repo's
             real one and cost a whole pass, because the reader saw an empty log and concluded
             nothing had been done.)
          2. ONLY if the dapr MCP is wired in this environment, ALSO call actor_state_set with
             actorId 'feature-hostile-fixture' and key 'plan', value the plan text — durable in the
             state store, inspectable from another session via actor_state_get. On the LOCAL
             substrate there are no services and so no dapr MCP: the file from (1) sits on the
             operator's own disk and is the whole handoff there, so skipping this is EXPECTED,
             not a degradation — do not attempt the call and do not report its absence.
          If (1) fails, or (2) fails while the tool IS present, do NOT fail the run — but you
          MUST say so explicitly in your final output, so a real degradation stays visible.
  
          Then implement the feature described under ===FEATURE SPEC=== below, following the
          plan. If the feature already appears implemented, make no changes and say so.
          COMMIT AS YOU GO on branch feature/hostile-fixture, at a sensible cadence — after each
          coherent unit of work (a migration, a module, one component), never after every file
          edit. Add files individually (never `git add -A` or `git commit -a`), and leave
          plan-feature-hostile-fixture.md uncommitted (run context, not feature code).
          Do NOT push the branch and do NOT open a pull request — this run stops at the local
          branch, and the operator decides what happens to it.
          (Committing costs the reader nothing: this is a worktree, so the change is
          `git diff <base>...HEAD` either way. Leaving it uncommitted only meant that a step
          killed at its wall-clock deadline lost everything it had paid for.)
  
          ===FEATURE SPEC===
          # Hostile-content fixture
          
          This spec deliberately carries every token class that must survive rendering untouched.
          It is the committed proof of the delimiter-coexistence story — if any layer (helm, YAML,
          the JSON wire step) mangles one of these, the golden snapshot diff catches it.
          
          - agent-side shell vars: $AGENT_APP_DIR and ${H_SKILLS_DIR}
          - envsubst-style tokens: ${VARS} and ${NOT_A_REAL_VAR}
          - engine-style tokens: {{step.field}} and {"$ref": "step.field"}
          - "double quotes", 'single quotes', back\slashes, a lone $ sign
          - markdown: `inline code`, **bold**, a [link](https://example.invalid)
          
            an indented block
              a deeper one
          
          Trailing punctuation and unicode: café, emoji ✅, em-dash —
          
  
          ===IMPLEMENTATION PLAN===
          {{plan.output}}
  
  '''
